---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/agriculture/intermediate/pests-weeds-and-ipm">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Integrated Pest Management is <strong>ecology substituting for calendar spraying</strong>:
    thresholds, prevention, biological control, and chemistry only as the last, best-targeted
    move. The three enemies are <strong>weeds</strong> (plants competing with the crop — the
    biggest yield thief, capable of taking more yield than insects and diseases combined),
    <strong>pests</strong> (animals eating the crop) and <strong>diseases</strong> (pathogens —
    fungi, bacteria, viruses, oomycetes). Each has its own biology, so each has its own
    control logic — but IPM's framework is one: measure, prevent, and intervene only when the
    economics say so. It is the agriculture lesson where the ecology ladder pays its rent.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="thresholds, prevention, beneficials, precise chemistry" widget="equationStep" config={ipmCfg} />

  <h2>Weed control: the competition problem</h2>
  <p>
    A weed is simply a plant growing where it competes with the crop — and its damage is
    pure resource theft: light, water and nitrogen captured by the wrong plant. The
    <strong>critical period</strong> concept drives the timing: crops tolerate early weed
    presence up to a point (the "competitive balance" date) and again after canopy closure;
    it is the window between — roughly the first third of the crop's life in most row
    crops — where competition costs yield. Controls stack in layers.
    <strong>Cultural</strong>: rotations that swap crop architecture and planting dates
    (soybeans following wheat disrupt a different weed spectrum), competitive varieties
    and narrower rows (canopy closure is a weed-suppression technology), stale seedbeds
    (germinate the flush, kill it, then drill). <strong>Physical</strong>: tillage and
    hoeing — effective, energy-costly, and soil-damaging in excess (the soil lesson's
    bill). <strong>Biological/chemical-adjacent</strong>: mulches, cover crops (rye's
    allelopathy genuinely suppresses seedling weeds), and in flooded rice the water
    itself. <strong>Chemical</strong>: herbicides, classed by mechanism — contact vs
    systemic, and by mode of action (photosystem inhibitors, growth regulators,
    the ALS and ACCase enzyme blockers). Herbicide use is dominated by one history:
    glyphosate (Roundup), broad-spectrum and low-toxicity, paired with engineered
    resistant crops from 1996 — a system whose overuse manufactured the resistance
    problem described below.
  </p>

  <h2>Pest control: the population problem</h2>
  <p>
    Insect pests are managed as populations, and the founding IPM insight (Stern and
    colleagues, 1959) was the <strong>economic threshold</strong>: <em>not every pest is
    worth spraying</em>. Below a density where damage cost exceeds control cost, spraying
    loses money twice — the chemical spend, and the beneficial insects killed while doing
    free work. Scouting fields (counts per plant, per sweep net) turns pest management
    from a calendar into a measurement discipline; threshold tables exist for the major
    crop–pest pairs. The control stack then runs: <strong>cultural</strong> (rotation
    starves specialists — corn rootworm's larvae simply die without corn; planting dates
    dodge peak flights; clean seed and sanitation deny overwintering sites),
    <strong>mechanical/physical</strong> (traps, pheromone confusion — saturating the air
    with synthetic sex pheromone so males cannot find females: mating disruption is the
    reason codling moth is manageable in orchards), <strong>biological</strong> (below),
    <strong>genetic</strong> (host-plant resistance bred or engineered — Bt crops express
    bacterial proteins lethal to specific caterpillars and beetles), and
    <strong>chemical</strong>, last: selective products, timed to the vulnerable life
    stage (larvae, not egg-laying adults; scale crawlers, not the armoured adults), and
    applied when bees are not foraging.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a bug seen means a bug spray." Most insects in a field
    are neutral or beneficial — predators, parasitoids, pollinators — and blanket spraying
    kills the pest's enemies faster than the pest, which is why the classic outcome of
    calendar spraying is <em>resurgent</em> pest outbreaks and secondary pests (spider
    mites, formerly suppressed by their own enemies, exploding after broad-spectrum
    sprays). The threshold discipline exists precisely to keep the field's own defence
    employed.</p>

  <h2>Biological control: employing the food web</h2>
  <p>
    Biological control is the ecology lesson's predation, contracted. Three modes:
    <strong>conservation biocontrol</strong> (feed and house the beneficials already there —
    beetle banks, hedgerows, flowering strips that give parasitoid wasps nectar; farms that
    spray less keep more of this workforce), <strong>augmentation</strong> (rearing and
    releasing — ladybirds against aphids, <em>Trichogramma</em> egg-parasitoid wasps against
    borers, predatory mites in greenhouses; the greenhouse industry now runs largely on
    purchased beneficials because it can), and <strong>classical biocontrol</strong>
    (importing an enemy against an introduced pest — the founding triumph being the
    cottony cushion scale in California, crushed in the 1890s by the imported vedalia
    beetle, an ongoing success for over a century). Classical releases carry the
    discipline's permanent caution: the cane toad (introduced to Australia, 1935) ate
    everything except the pest and became an invasive catastrophe — modern classical
    programmes run through quarantine screening and host-specificity testing for good
    reason. Biopesticides (<em>Bacillus thuringiensis</em> sprays, fungal and viral
    insecticides) bridge the chemical/biological gap: living or biologically derived,
    narrow-spectrum, resistance-manageable. The history of the field runs through one
    book: Rachel Carson's <em>Silent Spring</em> (1962) documented how broad-spectrum
    organochlorines (DDT) bioaccumulated up food chains — thinning raptor eggshells,
    killing non-targets, persisting for years — and its public impact created the
    environmental movement's regulatory wing and, indirectly, the professional space for
    IPM: the alternative to banned blanket chemistry had to be smarter, more targeted
    management, and IPM was the discipline waiting with the framework.
  </p>

  <h2>Disease control: the triangle and the rotation</h2>
  <p>
    Plant disease needs three vertices together — the <strong>disease triangle</strong>: a
    susceptible host, a virulent pathogen, and a favourable environment (usually moisture).
    Control attacks whichever vertex is cheapest. The <strong>host</strong>: resistant
    varieties (the breeding lesson's largest continuing gift — new rust resistance keeps
    wheat alive worldwide), certified disease-free seed and tubers. The
    <strong>environment</strong>: spacing and pruning for airflow, drip irrigation instead
    of overhead wetting (most fungal spores need wet leaves — the watering method is a
    disease decision), rotation out of host crops (potatoes every fourth year, not every
    year, or blight builds in the soil), sanitation (removing infected debris; the
    Irish famine's phytophthora survives between seasons in volunteer tubers). The
    <strong>pathogen</strong>: fungicides, protectant (contact — copper, sulfur, preventing
    spore germination) and systemic (entering tissue, curative), plus the hygiene
    rules that matter more than any product — cleaning boots and machinery between
    fields, because the pathogen travels on the farmer. Forecasting models (blight
    warnings from weather data) now schedule sprays by infection risk rather than
    calendar — IPM's threshold logic, applied to spores.
  </p>

  <h2>The resistance treadmill: evolution on a tractor</h2>
  <p>
    Every repeated control selects for survivors — resistance management is population
    genetics at field scale. The record: more than 500 arthropod species resist at least
    one insecticide; herbicide-resistant weeds now number in the hundreds of species-site
    combinations, with <em>Palmer amaranth</em> (waterhemp's fiercer cousin) forcing US
    cotton and soybean farms back to hand-hoeing and tillage in infested counties — a
    1990s solution re-adopted in the 2020s. Fungicide resistance tracks the same law
    (the strobilurin class's fall is a case study). The management principles are
    correspondingly genetic: <strong>rotate modes of action</strong> (alternating
    selection pressures — the IRAC and FRAC numbering systems exist so labels can say
    "Group 4 this year, Group 9 next"), <strong>mix</strong> products with different
    modes, <strong>refuge strategies</strong> for Bt crops (mandatory blocks of
    non-Bt corn whose susceptible pests dilute the resistance genes in the mating pool —
    evolution managed by geometry), <strong>integrated modes</strong> (cultural +
    mechanical + chemical), and <strong>the dose rule</strong>: under-dosing selects
    hardest of all, because the partially-resistant heterozygotes survive. Darwin
    collects his royalty on every overused mode of action.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked threshold: aphids on wheat</h2>
  <p>
    Make the threshold arithmetic concrete. Scouting finds an average of 12 aphids per
    wheat tiller in late spring; the advisory threshold for grain aphids sits around
    5–10 per tiller when predators are scarce, but rises when ladybird and parasitoid
    activity is visible. Suppose the count is below threshold and predators are present:
    spraying costs (chemical + application) roughly 20 €/ha; the expected yield saving
    from killing aphids already partly controlled by natural enemies is less than that —
    the spray is a net loss, and it deletes the parasitoids that would have carried the
    suppression through the next generation. Two weeks later the population has
    plateaued (the predators caught up) and no spray is ever spent. Reverse the case —
    20 aphids per tiller, predators absent, cold weather slowing the crop's own
    compensation — and the threshold is crossed with margin: one well-timed spray of a
    selective product (pirimicarb hits aphids and spares ladybirds; a broad-spectrum
    pyrethroid kills both) is the right move. Same field, same pest, opposite decisions —
    the difference is a count, a weather note, and a look for ladybird larvae. IPM in one
    paragraph.
  </p>

  <h2>Pesticides: classes, drift and safe use</h2>
  <p>
    The chemical shelf sorts by target and mechanism, and the sorting matters for
    everything from resistance to safety. Insecticides: organophosphates and carbamates
    (acetylcholinesterase inhibitors — older, acutely toxic, declining), pyrethroids
    (nerve poisons, broad-spectrum, cheap — but lethal to aquatic life at parts per
    billion), neonicotinoids (systemic, seed-treated — effective, and restricted in the
    EU over bee evidence), and the newer selective classes. Fungicides: protectants
    (copper, sulfur, chlorothalonil — surface barriers) versus systemics (entering the
    plant, curative but resistance-prone). Herbicides: the big groups by mode of action
    (glyphosate's EPSPS inhibition; the growth-regulator "hormone weedkillers" like
    2,4-D; photosystem blockers). Safe use is a regulated craft, not advice: label
    rates, protective equipment, buffer zones to water, pre-harvest intervals (the wait
    between last spray and picking, so residues decay below limits — the actual meaning
    of "residue-safe"), and storage locked away from food. <strong>Drift</strong> —
    spray moving off-target on wind — is the neighbour-relations and pollinator issue:
    low-drift nozzles, wind-speed rules and bee-safe timing (evening, not bloom) are
    the standard controls, and in many jurisdictions they are law. The regulatory
    backdrop has teeth: approval requires dossiers on toxicity, fate and residues, and
    products genuinely get withdrawn (the EU's neonic ban; chlorpyrifos) — pesticide law
    is where the environmental-science and agriculture lessons most directly share a
    file.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The economics of IPM explains its adoption curve. pesticides are the most convenient
    risk insurance in agriculture — one pass, one purchase, a season's apparent certainty —
    so the barrier to IPM is not knowledge but the accounting: thresholds require scouting
    labour; rotations require planning; biological control requires tolerance of
    below-threshold damage. Where those costs are internalised (advisory services,
    group scouting, insurance design), adoption follows — the EU's Sustainable Use
    Directive made IPM mandatory in principle, and the national "IPM packages" now being
    tested are mostly accounting devices. The frontier tools are arriving fast: gene
    editing for disease-resistant varieties (the late-blight-resistant potato is the
    showcase), RNAi sprays (species-specific gene silencing — a pesticide as precise as
    a sequence), sterile-insect programmes (screwworm eradicated from North America by
    aerial release of sterilised males — the only pest ever eliminated from a
    continent), and AI scouting on phones. And the deeper reframe continues: the
    field's best pesticide is often the field itself — its beetle bank, its flowering
    strip, its rotation — because IPM's founding insight was never anti-chemical; it
    was that the cheapest, most durable control is an ecosystem that does not need
    rescuing. Pollination closes the loop: a third of food crops depend on insect
    pollinators, so every spray window and every hedgerow is a hiring decision about
    the farm's most underpaid employees.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Stern et al. (1959) — the founding IPM paper; IRAC/FRAC resistance tables; the ecology and evolution lessons; Cambridge IGCSE Agriculture 0600, unit 5.</p>
</TopicPage>
